#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand classic confinement, the most permissive snap confinement level, "
"which grants applications broad system access and requires Snap Store "
"vetting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:8
msgid "Classic confinement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:10
msgid ""
"Classic confinement is the most permissive level of {ref}`snap confinement "
"<explanation-security-snap-confinement>`, equivalent to the full system "
"access that traditional, unsandboxed packages have. Unlike strict "
"confinement, classic confinement allows a snap to access the host system "
"directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:12
msgid ""
"Classic confinement has a very high maintenance cost. Since classic snaps "
"are not isolated from the host system, developers must ensure that all "
"binaries and libraries in their snap use the libraries, interpreters, and "
"dependencies shipped in the snap rather than those in the host system. "
"Before attempting to craft a classic snap, check wether its use case appears "
"in {ref}`the list of supported categories<interfaces-reviewing-classic-"
"confinement-snaps-supported>`. For details on crafting snaps with classic "
"confinement, see [Classic confinement](https://ubuntu.com/docs/snapcraft/"
"latest/explanation/classic-confinement/) in the Snapcraft documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:14
msgid ""
"This page provides explanations and examples of what happens during "
"installation and at runtime with snaps packaged using classic confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:16
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:18
msgid ""
"One of the main drives behind the invention of snaps was to guarantee safe "
"execution of software and mandate that packages abide by the principle of "
"least privilege. The snap package format places the software in a sandbox, "
"and snapd mediates all access to host system resources. By default, a snap "
"is strictly confined, where it only has host access that's explicitly "
"granted through the interface system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:20
msgid ""
"Apps can be packaged as classic snaps for a variety of reasons. Primarily, "
"this level of confinement is provided for apps that need access to arbitrary "
"binaries on the host, which isn't possible when using strict confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:22
msgid ""
"Apps packaged as classic snaps behave similar to software provided and "
"installed through the host's package manager, like Aptitude (`apt`) or Red "
"Hat Package Manager (`rpm`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:24
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:26
msgid ""
"From a security standpoint, classic snaps are inherently riskier than "
"standard snaps, as they lack the protections and guardrails offered by "
"sandboxing. A malicious author could easily compromise a system by "
"incorporating a compromising payload into a classic snap. And as Canonical "
"finds is frequently the case, not all requests for classic confinement are "
"necessary. Some authors misunderstand the snap build process, interfaces, or "
"filesystem, and with some tweaking their snap is made to work without "
"classic confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:28
msgid ""
"Canonical must therefore audit and vet all classic snaps prior to "
"distribution on any store. Without approval from the Store team, a classic "
"snap can't be distributed. The review process that determines the "
"eligibility and appropriateness as a classic snap is outlined in {ref}"
"`Submit your snap for review <interfaces-reviewing-classic-confinement-"
"snaps>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:30
msgid ""
"Canonical only manages risk for its own infrastructure and channels. If a "
"user obtains a classic snap through unofficial channels and they understand "
"the risks, they can manually install it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:32
msgid "Install-time process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:34
msgid ""
"When a classic snap is installed, _snapd_ will perform the following actions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:36
msgid ""
"![Snap confinement at install time](https://assets.ubuntu.com/v1/35306066-"
"confinement_02.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:36
msgid "Snap confinement at install time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:38
msgid "Mount the snap as a loopback device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:40
msgid "Skip the creation of the snap-specific private mount namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:42
msgid "Skip the configuration of the device cgroups."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:44
msgid ""
"Create a permissive AppArmor profile (which will be loaded in complain mode "
"at runtime). The profile is stored under `/var/lib/snapd/apparmor/profiles`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:46
msgid ""
"Create a permissive Seccomp profile (which will be parsed at runtime). The "
"profile is stored under `/var/lib/snapd/seccomp/bpf` and will contain a "
"`@unrestricted\\n` entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:48
msgid "Runtime process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:50
msgid ""
"When a classic snap is executed on the host system, snapd will perform the "
"following actions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:52
msgid ""
"![Snap confinement at run time](https://assets.ubuntu.com/v1/d4018ec4-"
"confinement_01.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:52
msgid "Snap confinement at run time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:54
msgid ""
"snapd runs a sub-process called `snap-confine`, which is responsible for "
"creating the necessary confinement for the snap (with the rules set during "
"the installation process)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:56
msgid ""
"The [AppArmor](https://ubuntu.com/server/docs/security-apparmor) profile "
"will be loaded in complain mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:58
msgid ""
"This can be confirmed by running `apparmor_status`, which lists the relevant "
"profiles in the complain section of the output, such as "
"`snap.XXX.hook.configure` and `snap.XXX.snapcraft`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:60
msgid ""
"For classic snaps, the permissive Seccomp profile will be parsed. This can "
"be confirmed by running `grep Seccomp /proc/PID/status`, with the value of "
"`0` indicating the process does not currently use any Seccomp filtering."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:62
msgid ""
"Load the dynamic library dependencies in the following order – staged "
"packages, `/snap/<base>` (with the relevant base specified in snap.yaml "
"derived from the developer edited snapcraft.yaml), and then the libraries in "
"the host environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/classic-confinement.md:64
msgid ""
"If not found, search through the host system using the system's `/etc/"
"ld.so.cache`. Classic snaps should not have a `$LD_LIBRARY_PATH` configured "
"as part of their runtime environment."
msgstr ""

